E20-S2|E20|On-call runbook and escalation path|ops|E16-S6,E17-S4|in_review|A-runbook|feat/E20-S2-runbook|a671f42|evidence/E20-S2.md||orchestrator|Drafted and merged as docs/ONCALL-RUNBOOK.md (54.9KB, 61 sections) covering all four failure classes with commands checked against live resource names: api down with the rollback-to-previous-revision procedure and the explicit warning that `aws ecs wait services-stable` returns success AFTER a circuit-breaker rollback, model throttling with what the user sees (SAFE_FALLBACK) and how to confirm from logs, database restore referencing the measured drill times, and secret rotation for the RDS password, the leaked Google OAuth secret and the therapist SSM passwords. Also documents alerting as it actually is: alarms reach SNS/SQS but page nobody because the email subscription is unconfirmed. HELD IN REVIEW, not verified: the acceptance requires the clinical escalation route to be approved by the clinical owner, which is a human signature that does not exist (E17-S7 not_started, the supervised-beta escalation contact table never filled). Section 6 ships under an UNAPPROVED-PENDING-CLINICAL-OWNER banner with a blank sign-off table. The one piece of logic that could silently corrupt data - the secret URI rewrite - was dry-run against the live secret payload; no live rollback, rotation or restore was executed, because proving a runbook that way would itself be an incident. This closes the E15-S3 runbook clause as drafted, pending that signature.
